236
00:19:18,861 --> 00:19:20,920
It's brittle.

237
00:19:20,921 --> 00:19:26,580
In 1856, British engineer Henry Bessemer found a way to make a stronger form of iron

238
00:19:26,581 --> 00:19:32,260
by combining it with carbon in a blast furnace to make steel.

239
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:36,340
Now with steel, you could cut it, you could mill it,

240
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:40,500
you could shape it in not only a tube, but also a breech-loading barrel.

241
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:44,880
With the addition of metallic cartridges, you could make a very successful

242
00:19:44,881 --> 00:19:48,040
not only breech-loading weapon, but a repeating weapon as well.

243
00:19:50,920 --> 00:20:00,980
In 1862, the Union and the Confederacy began armoring their warships during the American

244
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:03,520
Civil War.

245
00:20:03,521 --> 00:20:09,160
The iron clad ships, they were made from sheets of wrought iron.

246
00:20:09,161 --> 00:20:19,320
These sheets were applied either to a wooden hull ship, such as the Merrimack, or the entire

247
00:20:19,321 --> 00:20:22,320
The entire ship was made of sheets of wrought iron

248
00:20:22,321 --> 00:20:25,320
that were riveted together to form the hull

249
00:20:25,321 --> 00:20:28,320
and a turret on top of the deck,

250
00:20:28,321 --> 00:20:30,320
as in the case of the Monitor.

251
00:20:30,321 --> 00:20:32,320
In their one and only battle,

252
00:20:32,321 --> 00:20:35,320
both ships received dozens of direct hits,

253
00:20:35,321 --> 00:20:37,320
but remained afloat.

254
00:20:37,321 --> 00:20:40,320
In the end, the battle was a draw.

255
00:20:40,321 --> 00:20:43,320
Iron proved to be the real winner,

256
00:20:43,321 --> 00:20:46,320
changing naval warfare forever.

257
00:20:46,321 --> 00:20:52,360
By the 20th century, iron-rich hematite supplied America's vast steel industry.

258
00:20:53,540 --> 00:20:58,400
In 1915, steel supported a new, mechanized war.

259
00:20:59,320 --> 00:21:02,320
The tanks of World War I were known as land ships.

260
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:06,640
Just like the ironclads of the Civil War revolutionized naval warfare,

261
00:21:07,080 --> 00:21:10,800
these land ships of all steel revolutionized land warfare.

262
00:21:10,801 --> 00:21:18,180
The iron battlefield helmet, which first appeared centuries ago, was reintroduced

263
00:21:18,181 --> 00:21:23,480
during World War I, this time in the form of steel.

264
00:21:23,481 --> 00:21:28,520
This is an original World War I helmet as worn by the British and United States troops.

265
00:21:28,521 --> 00:21:30,800
It's made out of thin sheet steel.

266
00:21:30,801 --> 00:21:33,900
It's been stamped the same way you would see a car body.

267
00:21:33,901 --> 00:21:36,180
The idea here is not to protect from bullets.

268
00:21:36,181 --> 00:21:42,120
It is to protect from iron shell fragments that have been tossed down from exploding shells from above.

269
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:46,700
You see, most soldiers spent four years fighting in trenches dug in the ground.

270
00:21:48,180 --> 00:21:51,940
World War II consumed nearly the last of the hematite iron.

271
00:21:58,280 --> 00:22:03,120
More than 65 million tons of ore was produced in 1944 alone.

272
00:22:03,121 --> 00:22:09,960
allowing American steel to rule the battlefield, the oceans, and even the skies.

273
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:15,820
It began to use all metal airplanes instead of those made of wood with a canvas fabric coating.

274
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:21,640
Steel began to replace all other organic materials for weapons.

275
00:22:24,220 --> 00:22:28,080
Today, iron is still the most important metal in every nation's military,

276
00:22:28,460 --> 00:22:30,960
as the principal component in all forms of steel.

277
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:39,240
But one configuration of iron as steel has proven to be warfare's most dangerous weapon,

278
00:22:39,241 --> 00:22:43,460
the barrel of a gun.

279
00:22:43,461 --> 00:22:50,940
The M16 rifle became the US Army's standard issue weapon in the Vietnam era.

280
00:22:50,941 --> 00:22:56,200
Its plastic, aluminum and steel construction is designed to be lightweight and portable

281
00:22:56,201 --> 00:22:57,460
in combat conditions.

282
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:04,960
But to achieve pinpoint accuracy,

283
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:09,740
gunsmiths at Fort Benning, Georgia,

284
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:11,320
use a heavier steel.

285
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:14,080
Getting rid of the plastic,

286
00:23:14,400 --> 00:23:15,580
aluminum, when you can,

287
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:17,740
you end up with a much better product.

288
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:19,660
And adding an iron to the rifle

289
00:23:19,661 --> 00:23:21,600
makes it a much better rifle,

290
00:23:21,601 --> 00:23:24,180
a much more accurate

291
00:23:24,181 --> 00:23:25,700
and much more precise.

292
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:33,200
They beef up the M16 rifle for the elite army marksmanship unit.

293
00:23:33,201 --> 00:23:39,200
Take a standard issue M16 and we basically rebuild it starting at one end to the other.

294
00:23:39,201 --> 00:23:44,200
The M16 barrel is reinforced with high grade chrome moly steel.

295
00:23:44,201 --> 00:23:52,200
It's made of over 85% iron and a critical 1% chromium and .2% molybdenum.

296
00:23:52,201 --> 00:24:03,340
Iron is the perfect metal for making rifle barrels due to the fact it will withstand high temperature and pressures exerted from the bullet.

297
00:24:03,820 --> 00:24:07,460
It will last for a much longer time than some other metals.

298
00:24:11,260 --> 00:24:21,220
With each shot, the barrel must stand up to the force of a bullet speeding 3,000 feet per second with a pressure of 65,000 pounds per square inch.

299
00:24:22,200 --> 00:24:35,640
The Army fashions the modified M16 barrel from a piece of raw stock called a blank.

300
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:41,740
What we're starting with is a nine-pound rifled blank to make an M16 barrel.

301
00:24:43,700 --> 00:24:46,580
We have to machine the exterior profile.

302
00:24:47,640 --> 00:24:52,180
It's going to end up being around four pounds, so we'll take about half that weight off.

303
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:56,300
You've got areas that need to be within 1 10,000th of an inch,

304
00:24:56,440 --> 00:25:01,060
which equates to 1 40th of the thickness of a sheet of notebook paper.

305
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:05,680
And those tolerances have to be extremely tight for accuracy and reliability.

306
00:25:09,840 --> 00:25:12,180
After rigorous refinements and inspections,

307
00:25:12,560 --> 00:25:17,420
the modified M16 rifle is ready to challenge the standard issue on the firing line.

308
00:25:19,180 --> 00:25:21,480
This is the standard M16 rifle.

309
00:25:22,200 --> 00:25:29,200
This is our modified Marksman M16 rifle.

310
00:25:29,201 --> 00:25:32,200
As you can tell by its custom made barrel.

311
00:25:41,200 --> 00:25:43,200
This target was shot with a standard M16 rifle,

312
00:25:43,201 --> 00:25:46,200
and it's about a 7-inch diameter group.

313
00:25:46,201 --> 00:25:50,200
This target was shot with a modified match M16,

314
00:25:50,201 --> 00:25:52,400
and it's about a 1 1⁄2 inch group.

315
00:25:56,100 --> 00:25:58,460
Although it has been synonymous with war,

316
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:01,220
iron is invaluable to modern society.

317
00:26:05,580 --> 00:26:07,940
One of iron's most curious properties

318
00:26:07,941 --> 00:26:11,440
provides the motion that powers the machines of our world.

319
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:13,180
It's magnetic.

320
00:26:16,920 --> 00:26:19,900
Vagons are so important to the way the world works.

321
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:24,740
The forces between magnets, the attractions and repulsions, can make things move.

322
00:26:27,420 --> 00:26:32,220
This is a supermagnet made of iron combined with a number of other elements,

323
00:26:33,460 --> 00:26:35,620
boron and neodymium.

324
00:26:36,100 --> 00:26:39,020
It is 100 times stronger than the magnet on your refrigerator.

325
00:26:40,880 --> 00:26:42,460
Supermagnets can be very dangerous.

326
00:26:42,920 --> 00:26:45,220
They exert such enormous forces on one another

327
00:26:45,221 --> 00:26:48,560
that if your hand is in the way when they leap, you're in trouble.

328
00:26:48,561 --> 00:26:52,800
I've put one supermagnet inside this wine glass,

329
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,420
and I'm going to bring the other supermagnet up toward it.

330
00:26:58,120 --> 00:27:01,480
This attractive wonder is the so-called permanent magnet,

331
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:04,560
an iron-bearing metal that retains its magnetism

332
00:27:04,561 --> 00:27:06,920
after the removal of the magnetic force.

333
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:11,460
That force can either be another permanent magnet

334
00:27:11,461 --> 00:27:13,920
or a jolt of electricity.

335
00:27:13,921 --> 00:27:22,860
another form of magnet an electromagnet is magnetic only when energized by electric current

336
00:27:22,861 --> 00:27:27,640
combine electricity with any piece of iron and you've got an electromagnet

337
00:27:27,641 --> 00:27:36,320
this is a piece of ordinary iron it's not magnetic it won't pick up the other bolts

338
00:27:36,321 --> 00:27:41,560
To make it magnetic, I'm going to wrap this wire around this bar.

339
00:27:42,940 --> 00:27:50,600
Now, when I run electric current through this coil of wire, it will turn into a magnet, an electromagnet.

340
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:55,440
And now, it picks up the iron.

341
00:27:56,700 --> 00:28:00,660
But if I cut the power, magnetism goes away.

342
00:28:01,400 --> 00:28:06,300
This property is the key to movement in electric motors that power our industry.

343
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:16,840
An electromagnet called the armature revolves between the north and south poles of a stationary permanent magnet called the stator.

344
00:28:17,840 --> 00:28:22,780
The armature rotates until its north pole is opposite the south pole of the stator.

345
00:28:24,580 --> 00:28:27,700
The direction of the armature's current is then reversed.

346
00:28:27,701 --> 00:28:34,180
The two south poles repel each other, pushing the armature forward.

347
00:28:34,181 --> 00:28:41,700
The current reverses every half turn, keeping the armature in motion.

348
00:28:41,701 --> 00:28:47,320
Iron is not only an integral part of the machinery powering our world, but also a mineral nutrient

349
00:28:47,321 --> 00:28:51,780
within each of us, crucial to our biology.

350
00:28:51,781 --> 00:28:57,020
The same iron that everybody sees around the world is the same iron within us.

351
00:28:57,021 --> 00:28:59,680
as small microscopic particles.

352
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:04,120
And without this iron, we will cease to live.

353
00:29:04,121 --> 00:29:07,060
We help maintain our body's vital supply of iron

354
00:29:07,061 --> 00:29:12,420
by ingesting iron contained in meat and from vegetables.

355
00:29:12,421 --> 00:29:15,080
Another source is certain brands of fortified breakfast

356
00:29:15,081 --> 00:29:20,260
cereals, which contain iron baked right into the flakes.

357
00:29:20,261 --> 00:29:21,820
There actually is a scientific way

358
00:29:21,821 --> 00:29:26,100
of determining the amount of iron present in a fortified cereal.

359
00:29:26,101 --> 00:29:28,600
All it takes is grinding up the flakes into a mash

360
00:29:28,601 --> 00:29:31,940
and using a magnetic stirring bar.

361
00:29:34,280 --> 00:29:37,340
The iron in the cereal is attracted to the magnetic bar.

362
00:29:39,580 --> 00:29:41,320
Then, a quick rinse.

363
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:44,940
There's about 8 milligrams of iron present in a bowl.

364
00:29:45,420 --> 00:29:47,720
When you start the day with a fortified breakfast cereal,

365
00:29:47,940 --> 00:29:50,200
you're actually eating pure iron filings.

366
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:53,560
Whatever its source,

367
00:29:53,561 --> 00:29:57,160
Iron's critical mission is transporting oxygen through our bodies.

368
00:29:58,140 --> 00:30:01,640
Normally, combining iron and oxygen produces iron oxide,

369
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:04,180
sometimes known as rust,

370
00:30:04,760 --> 00:30:07,920
which, no surprise, could do our bodies great damage.

371
00:30:11,220 --> 00:30:13,940
But iron, which exists in our red blood cells,

372
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:16,080
latches on to oxygen molecules.

373
00:30:16,840 --> 00:30:18,720
So why doesn't it turn to rust?

374
00:30:19,180 --> 00:30:20,900
The body's intriguing solution

375
00:30:20,901 --> 00:30:25,000
is to place the iron in a cage-like molecule called heme.

376
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:31,020
Heme is a beautiful cage that is made inside our body.

377
00:30:31,680 --> 00:30:35,120
It captures and encloses the iron in the center

378
00:30:35,121 --> 00:30:39,360
and prevents it from turning to rust when it binds to oxygen.

379
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:44,100
The iron and oxygen inside the heme

380
00:30:44,101 --> 00:30:46,300
attaches to a protein called globin.

381
00:30:46,301 --> 00:30:53,300
The new molecule, hemoglobin, travels in the blood, delivering oxygen throughout the body.

382
00:30:57,300 --> 00:31:01,300
But the genes that control this iron-dependent transportation are elusive.

383
00:31:02,300 --> 00:31:12,978
Discovering them is critical, because estimates are 1.6 billion people, about a quarter of the world's population, are anemic, lacking sufficient supplies of iron and

384
00:31:12,979 --> 00:31:13,679
heme.

385
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:15,300
and heme.

386
00:31:15,301 --> 00:31:18,100
Anemia can weaken the body's immune system

387
00:31:18,101 --> 00:31:20,300
and trigger life-threatening infections and diseases.

388
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,420
This microscopic transparent worm

389
00:31:25,421 --> 00:31:28,300
may be the key to unlocking the mystery.

390
00:31:28,301 --> 00:31:31,840
70% of its genes are identical to ours.

391
00:31:31,841 --> 00:31:36,860
And like us, it needs iron and heme to survive.

392
00:31:36,861 --> 00:31:40,920
So what we did was we took a small amount of iron and heme,

393
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:46,060
and put it on this petri dish.

394
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:56,520
So we can directly look at now what happens in a transparent animal

395
00:31:56,521 --> 00:32:00,800
as they're actively feeding on this iron and heme.

396
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:07,440
Dr. Hamza conducted experiments that revealed some of the worms digested iron

397
00:32:07,441 --> 00:32:08,980
less successfully than others.

398
00:32:08,981 --> 00:32:16,420
He went on to identify the missing or mutated genes of those worms, marking a major breakthrough.

399
00:32:17,620 --> 00:32:27,680
We were ecstatic because this will now provide us with a small clue as to how we transport iron and heme.

400
00:32:27,880 --> 00:32:33,180
This will provide us with a missing link between iron deficiency and anemia.

401
00:32:33,181 --> 00:32:37,480
With heme, the body may know how to avoid rust,

402
00:32:38,120 --> 00:32:40,080
but rust is everywhere in our environment.

403
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:44,960
Welcome back to Modern Marvels.

404
00:32:47,920 --> 00:32:49,780
Iron's hallmark is its strength.

405
00:32:51,160 --> 00:32:54,680
But this indispensable metal girding modern civilization

406
00:32:54,681 --> 00:32:56,740
has one key weakness.

407
00:32:57,120 --> 00:32:59,680
When exposed to air or water,

408
00:33:01,280 --> 00:33:03,040
iron turns to rust.

409
00:33:03,180 --> 00:33:08,180
We don't like rust because it weakens our structures, our buildings, our bridges,

410
00:33:08,181 --> 00:33:10,180
and it's quite a serious problem.

411
00:33:10,181 --> 00:33:16,180
It's a multi-multi-million dollar industry trying to control it, trying to stop it from happening.

412
00:33:18,180 --> 00:33:25,180
Rust is the result of a chemical reaction in which the 26 electrons within the iron atom again come into play.

413
00:33:26,180 --> 00:33:30,180
Two of those electrons orbit the nucleus farther away than the rest.

414
00:33:30,181 --> 00:33:31,720
than the rest.

415
00:33:31,721 --> 00:33:34,180
These electrons are sort of like black sheep.

416
00:33:34,181 --> 00:33:36,400
It would make the iron molecule a lot more stable

417
00:33:36,401 --> 00:33:39,220
if these electrons would go away, basically.

418
00:33:39,221 --> 00:33:42,940
Oxygen, on the other hand, needs a couple of electrons.

419
00:33:42,941 --> 00:33:44,100
So it's very eager to take them.

420
00:33:44,101 --> 00:33:47,080
So what happens is they come together, they combine,

421
00:33:47,081 --> 00:33:51,860
and they form a new compound called iron oxide.

422
00:33:51,861 --> 00:33:55,160
And that's iron's arch enemy, rust.

423
00:33:55,161 --> 00:33:58,320
Since the added oxygen makes the iron oxide molecule

424
00:33:58,321 --> 00:34:00,940
larger than the parent iron molecule,

425
00:34:00,941 --> 00:34:03,500
the rust displaces the iron and is clearly

426
00:34:03,501 --> 00:34:05,700
visible on the surface.

427
00:34:05,701 --> 00:34:08,220
The main things that we can do to protect surfaces

428
00:34:08,221 --> 00:34:14,600
from rusting are to coat them and stop

429
00:34:14,601 --> 00:34:17,960
the ability of oxygen and water to get to the surface

430
00:34:17,961 --> 00:34:20,280
in the first place.

431
00:34:20,281 --> 00:34:23,460
However, there are some who don't mind a little rust.

432
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:26,080
In fact, they want all they can get.

433
00:34:26,081 --> 00:34:26,781
We love it.

434
00:34:26,782 --> 00:34:27,860
It's rust is us.

435
00:34:32,640 --> 00:34:35,960
Rockwood Pigments, now owned by Venator Corporation,

436
00:34:36,620 --> 00:34:42,240
is a manufacturer that takes advantage of iron oxide's beneficial ability to color our world.

437
00:34:42,940 --> 00:34:48,640
Nature crafts iron oxide, the same stuff dug up by iron miners in a variety of earth tones.

438
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:55,860
Then, manufacturers take iron oxide derived from leftover iron and turn it into a variety of pigments.

439
00:34:55,861 --> 00:35:06,540
We take forms of iron mostly in scrap steel and oxidize it in sulfuric acid and then control that reaction to make different forms of iron oxide.

440
00:35:10,420 --> 00:35:16,000
One ton of scrap metal makes a ton and a half of pigment because of the extra oxygen molecule.

441
00:35:16,001 --> 00:35:25,880
Iron oxide arrives at the Rockwood Pigments Los Angeles plant every year in three basic

442
00:35:25,881 --> 00:35:29,240
colors, red, yellow, and black.

443
00:35:30,240 --> 00:35:32,460
These are two bulk sacks of iron oxide.

444
00:35:32,720 --> 00:35:33,660
Each one weighs a ton.

445
00:35:33,800 --> 00:35:35,320
Together, it's as much as an SUV.

446
00:35:36,940 --> 00:35:42,000
Various combinations of the three pigments enter a blender, where up to five tons can

447
00:35:42,001 --> 00:35:43,040
mix at one time.

448
00:35:43,041 --> 00:35:48,540
Our main concern here is to make sure that all the red, yellow and black, or the combination

449
00:35:48,541 --> 00:35:54,160
of pigments, are mixed together so that we don't get streaks in the finished product.

450
00:35:54,161 --> 00:35:58,820
In the quality control lab, workers mix the powdered pigment with a liquid binder of clay

451
00:35:58,821 --> 00:36:00,860
and water.

452
00:36:00,861 --> 00:36:06,920
This sample batch is then compared to the standard color to ensure consistency.

453
00:36:06,921 --> 00:36:08,800
This is the standard which we know is good.

454
00:36:08,801 --> 00:36:10,500
This is the batch which we're making.

455
00:36:10,501 --> 00:36:13,100
Obviously, we'll be adding some yellow shade red

456
00:36:13,101 --> 00:36:15,040
and sending it back to production for a rework.

457
00:36:15,700 --> 00:36:17,720
This is our standard again and our batch.

458
00:36:18,040 --> 00:36:19,580
Everything looks good after adjustment,

459
00:36:19,820 --> 00:36:21,020
ready to ship to the customer.

460
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:25,320
The idea of using iron oxide as pigment

461
00:36:25,321 --> 00:36:27,320
is as old as history itself.

462
00:36:28,160 --> 00:36:30,020
In fact, it was used to record history

463
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:33,040
as far back as 17,000 years ago.

464
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:37,300
Today, iron oxide paintings still endure

465
00:36:37,301 --> 00:36:39,780
and France's renowned Lescaux Cave.

466
00:36:40,500 --> 00:36:44,060
The basic concept endured for centuries as well.

467
00:36:47,200 --> 00:36:54,640
And late medieval and renaissance artists used iron oxide pigment for their rich, warm red and orange colors.

468
00:36:55,300 --> 00:37:01,200
Everyone from Michelangelo to da Vinci to Rembrandt and beyond have used iron oxide pigments.

469
00:37:03,320 --> 00:37:09,080
However, it turns out most of those pigments end up not on a canvas, but in concrete.

470
00:37:10,500 --> 00:37:13,380
Most people wouldn't know that concrete is colored.

471
00:37:13,860 --> 00:37:18,120
For instance, the floors you walk on, the concrete block that are structures,

472
00:37:18,580 --> 00:37:23,480
the pavers that are in our homes, roof tile on the top of homes,

473
00:37:23,481 --> 00:37:27,480
all of these are concrete applications that have been colored with iron oxide.

474
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:33,040
Any year, Rockwood Pigments makes enough iron oxide that would color concrete that could cover the state of Texas.

475
00:37:34,340 --> 00:37:40,040
But your closest encounter with one of these iron oxide pigments comes every time you open your wallet.

476
00:37:40,500 --> 00:37:46,375
We make hundreds of tons of black pigment that gets used in the color for the U.S.

477
00:37:46,376 --> 00:37:47,076
currency.

478
00:37:47,077 --> 00:37:50,880
This black pigment is so concentrated it makes millions and millions of bills.

479
00:37:50,881 --> 00:37:55,640
Iron oxide black is used in the ink that goes on the dollar bill because it doesn't fade,

480
00:37:55,641 --> 00:38:03,460
it's light fast, durable, and it will last a long time.

481
00:38:03,461 --> 00:38:08,380
Our money may come and go, but because of iron, Honest Abe will never fade.

482
00:38:10,500 --> 00:38:21,900
In February 2021, NASA achieved an incredible goal when the rover Perseverance landed on Mars.

483
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:28,380
Its primary mission is to determine if evidence can be found that life once existed on the red planet.

484
00:38:29,860 --> 00:38:34,940
But for some scientists, Mars holds another, completely different treasure.

485
00:38:34,941 --> 00:38:38,580
Mars is covered with rust.

486
00:38:39,260 --> 00:38:40,940
Mars is known as the Red Planet

487
00:38:40,941 --> 00:38:44,720
because of the iron oxides present in the Martian soil.

488
00:38:46,420 --> 00:38:49,660
There's probably billions of tons of iron on Mars

489
00:38:49,661 --> 00:38:52,320
that would satisfy civilization for hundreds of years,

490
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:53,520
if not thousands of years.

491
00:38:54,060 --> 00:38:56,900
While the Earth's crust is about 5% iron,

492
00:38:58,040 --> 00:39:01,400
the crust of Mars contains about 14%.

493
00:39:01,401 --> 00:39:08,400
This vast resource beckons the futurists and scientists thinking of ways to mine iron

494
00:39:08,401 --> 00:39:11,400
should we one day establish colonies on Mars.

495
00:39:11,401 --> 00:39:19,400
My role as a geologist is to find enough minerals for us to have a society to live on.

496
00:39:19,401 --> 00:39:25,400
Some estimate that within the next 50 years, human colonies may be established on Mars,

497
00:39:25,401 --> 00:39:29,400
where mining would be a breeze compared to the process on Earth.

498
00:39:29,401 --> 00:39:31,140
On Earth, for us to get to the iron,

499
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:33,540
we have to create these gigantic open pits

500
00:39:33,541 --> 00:39:36,600
that you see in front of us, where they go down hundreds of feet

501
00:39:36,601 --> 00:39:39,640
just to get to the iron, and they cover hundreds of square miles

502
00:39:39,641 --> 00:39:42,460
in the end, whereas on Mars, all of the iron ore

503
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:44,060
is sitting right on top of the surface,

504
00:39:44,160 --> 00:39:45,980
and although it covers hundreds of square miles,

505
00:39:46,160 --> 00:39:48,740
we don't have to dig down very deep, maybe about 18 inches,

506
00:39:48,820 --> 00:39:50,880
so that's a lot different than what you see in front of us here.

507
00:39:52,520 --> 00:39:55,840
The Martian surface may prove to be even more user-friendly

508
00:39:55,841 --> 00:39:56,541
than imagined.

509
00:39:57,280 --> 00:40:00,000
The pictures sent back from various Mars rover missions

510
00:40:00,001 --> 00:40:05,020
suggest that natural processes have already accomplished a key part of the mining.

511
00:40:05,940 --> 00:40:10,120
When the probe landed on the Mars surface and started roaming around,

512
00:40:10,180 --> 00:40:12,220
they started noticing all these little round pellets,

513
00:40:12,280 --> 00:40:14,120
and they were wondering, what in the world is that?

514
00:40:14,260 --> 00:40:16,720
And they discovered that they were actually iron minerals.

515
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:21,880
Scientists believe those pellets contain amounts of iron similar to the man-made pellets

516
00:40:21,881 --> 00:40:26,680
that need to be mined, crushed, and baked here on Earth.

517
00:40:28,660 --> 00:40:31,520
What's really good about the iron ore deposits on Mars

518
00:40:31,521 --> 00:40:32,720
is they're already in pellets.

519
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:34,460
We can go ahead and put them in a blast furnace.

520
00:40:35,980 --> 00:40:38,840
Martian mining offers one further advantage over Earth.

521
00:40:40,220 --> 00:40:43,540
Gravity on Mars is about 38% of what it is on Earth,

522
00:40:43,620 --> 00:40:45,040
so everything is a lot lighter.

523
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:46,960
We don't need the big, heavy machinery

524
00:40:46,961 --> 00:40:48,600
because everything is on the surface.

525
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:51,480
All we have to do is just scoop up the iron minerals,

526
00:40:51,481 --> 00:40:54,720
and transport the iron minerals to our community

527
00:40:54,721 --> 00:40:56,120
where we'll convert it into iron metal.

528
00:40:57,020 --> 00:40:59,820
The iron mine would be refined on Mars itself

529
00:40:59,821 --> 00:41:03,540
to help build the infrastructure of extraterrestrial colonies

530
00:41:03,541 --> 00:41:05,880
on Mars and beyond.

531
00:41:07,700 --> 00:41:10,580
So we want to find it on Mars and use it there.

532
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:13,380
And once we start manufacturing the products,

533
00:41:13,520 --> 00:41:16,100
then we can take it into the other heavenly bodies.

534
00:41:16,360 --> 00:41:20,620
This is like a way station for our expansion of our people into space.

535
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:24,760
As rich as the treasure of iron on Mars is,

536
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:28,440
the true mother load is on asteroids.

537
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:33,240
Some scientists believe they're comprised of up to 98% iron.

538
00:41:34,060 --> 00:41:39,500
And these asteroids are so big that they actually have more iron than we've ever produced on planet Earth.

539
00:41:40,960 --> 00:41:46,100
Only time will tell if dreams of mining this essential metal in the heavens will come true.

540
00:41:46,960 --> 00:41:49,980
But even if our only source remains here on Earth,

541
00:41:49,981 --> 00:41:54,980
Our need for iron promises to endure as long as we do.

542
00:41:55,980 --> 00:41:57,980
We'll always have iron.

543
00:41:57,981 --> 00:41:59,980
Iron will always be a central part of our lives.

544
00:41:59,981 --> 00:42:02,980
It's part of our structures. It's part of our motion.

545
00:42:02,981 --> 00:42:05,980
It's part of us. We'll never be without iron.

